  {"type": "split", "photo": "sys", "h2": "Why a static pressure test comes before any Thornhill furnace quote",
   "html": "<p>Static pressure is the resistance the blower pushes against to move air through your ducts. We measure it with a manometer at the furnace, on the supply side and the return side, before we size anything.</p>"
           "<p>In a 1970s or 1980s Thornhill house the reading is often well above what the new furnace is built for. That tells us the ducts, not the furnace, are the bottleneck. A bigger furnace would make it worse. The right answer is usually a second return, a larger filter cabinet or a short section of trunk opened up, and then a furnace sized to the house's real heat loss.</p>"
           "<p>The test takes a few minutes and the numbers go on your quote, so you can compare them to the readings we take again after the duct work is done.</p>"},
